
DB Schema and code example for a service which maintains users, user can have multiple roles and role inturn can have multiple permissions. The schema and code should be flexible/extendable such that we can add new permissions under a role and similarly new role to a user

Below is a clean, extensible, industry-standard RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) design that supports:
* Users with multiple roles
* Roles with multiple permissions
* Easy addition of new roles and new permissions without schema changes
* Works well with SQL + any backend language / ORM

1. Database Schema (Normalized & Extensible)
Core Entities
User ──< UserRole >── Role ──< RolePermission >── Permission

1.1 Users Table
CREATE TABLE users (
    id              UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    username        VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    email           VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    password_hash   VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    is_active       BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE,
    created_at      TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

1.2 Roles Table
CREATE TABLE roles (
    id          UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    name        VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    description TEXT,
    created_at  TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);
Examples:
* ADMIN
* EDITOR
* VIEWER

1.3 Permissions Table
CREATE TABLE permissions (
    id          UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    name        VARCHAR(150) UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    description TEXT,
    created_at  TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);
Examples:
* user.create
* user.delete
* article.publish
* article.read
💡 Using dot-notation keeps permissions extensible and readable.

1.4 User ↔ Role Mapping (Many-to-Many)
CREATE TABLE user_roles (
    user_id UUID NOT NULL,
    role_id UUID NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, role_id),
    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (role_id) REFERENCES roles(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);

1.5 Role ↔ Permission Mapping (Many-to-Many)
CREATE TABLE role_permissions (
    role_id        UUID NOT NULL,
    permission_id  UUID NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (role_id, permission_id),
    FOREIGN KEY (role_id) REFERENCES roles(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (permission_id) REFERENCES permissions(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);

2. Why This Schema Is Flexible
✔ Add a new permission → insert into permissions, map to roles ✔ Add a new role → insert into roles, map permissions ✔ Assign multiple roles to users ✔ No schema change needed when business rules evolve
This is how most enterprise systems (AWS IAM, Keycloak, Auth0, Spring Security) work internally.

3. Example Data
Create Permissions
INSERT INTO permissions (id, name)
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'user.create'),
(gen_random_uuid(), 'user.delete'),
(gen_random_uuid(), 'article.read'),
(gen_random_uuid(), 'article.publish');

Create Role
INSERT INTO roles (id, name)
VALUES (gen_random_uuid(), 'ADMIN');

Assign Permissions to Role
INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_id, permission_id)
SELECT r.id, p.id
FROM roles r, permissions p
WHERE r.name = 'ADMIN'
  AND p.name IN ('user.create', 'user.delete', 'article.publish');

Assign Role to User
INSERT INTO user_roles (user_id, role_id)
VALUES ('<USER_UUID>', '<ROLE_UUID>');

4. Code Example (Service Layer – Pseudo / Java-like)
4.1 Check if User Has a Permission
boolean hasPermission(UUID userId, String permissionName) {
    return jdbcTemplate.queryForObject("""
        SELECT EXISTS (
            SELECT 1
            FROM user_roles ur
            JOIN role_permissions rp ON ur.role_id = rp.role_id
            JOIN permissions p ON rp.permission_id = p.id
            WHERE ur.user_id = ?
              AND p.name = ?
        )
    """, Boolean.class, userId, permissionName);
}
Usage:
if (hasPermission(userId, "article.publish")) {
    publishArticle();
}

4.2 Assign Role to User
void assignRoleToUser(UUID userId, UUID roleId) {
    jdbcTemplate.update("""
        INSERT INTO user_roles (user_id, role_id)
        VALUES (?, ?)
        ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING
    """, userId, roleId);
}

4.3 Add Permission to Role
void addPermissionToRole(UUID roleId, UUID permissionId) {
    jdbcTemplate.update("""
        INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_id, permission_id)
        VALUES (?, ?)
        ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING
    """, roleId, permissionId);
}

5. Optional Extensions (Common in Real Systems)
🔹 Role Hierarchy
Add:
parent_role_id UUID NULL REFERENCES roles(id)
🔹 Direct User Permissions (Overrides)
user_permissions(user_id, permission_id)
🔹 Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC)
Add conditions like:
{
  "resource": "article",
  "condition": "owner == user.id"
}

